    "group": "201-300",
    "title": "Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Tree",
    "workload": "build one 100000-node complete binary tree as an index pool, then 600 full recursive post-order LCA searches. Contrast with #235: that kata's BST ordering lets the walk pick a direction and finish in O(h)~35 steps, so it needs 8000000 queries for a comparable runtime; here there is no ordering to exploit, the recursion must inspect BOTH subtrees, and the cost is O(n) per query -- the same problem shape three orders of magnitude apart in per-query cost. A complete tree keeps recursion depth at ~17. Sink accumulates LCA values mod 1e9+7. NOTE: all four lanes use an index-pool tree rather than pointer-linked nodes.",
